   "type": "timeseries",
   "title": "Mongo index drift",
   "datasource": {
    "type": "prometheus",
    "uid": "${datasource}"
   },
   "gridPos": {
    "x": 0,
    "y": 97,
    "w": 12,
    "h": 8
   },
   "targets": [
    {
     "refId": "A",
     "datasource": {
      "type": "prometheus",
      "uid": "${datasource}"
     },
     "expr": "max(mongo_index_unknown)",
     "legendFormat": "undeclared indexes"
    },
    {
     "refId": "B",
     "datasource": {
      "type": "prometheus",
      "uid": "${datasource}"
     },
     "expr": "max(mongo_index_reconcile_failed)",
     "legendFormat": "failed to rebuild"
    },
    {
     "refId": "C",
     "datasource": {
      "type": "prometheus",
      "uid": "${datasource}"
     },
     "expr": "sum(increase(mongo_index_rebuilt_total[24h]))",
     "legendFormat": "rebuilt (24h)"
    }
   ],
   "fieldConfig": {
    "defaults": {},
    "overrides": []
   },
   "options": {
    "legend": {
     "displayMode": "list",
     "placement": "bottom",
     "showLegend": true
    },
    "tooltip": {
     "mode": "multi",
     "sort": "desc"
    }
   },
   "description": "Set ONCE per process, before the instance serves traffic, so these hold their boot value for the life of a pod. `failed to rebuild` is alerted (MongoIndexReconcileFailed) because the old definition stays in place and writes can keep failing against it. `undeclared indexes` is deliberately NOT alerted — it is usually an operator adding an index by hand to rescue a slow query, and index-reconcile.ts reports those and leaves them alone."
  },
